  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is centered off the West Coast. An upper level \ndisturbance/shortwave is over portions of north central Kansas and \nsouth central Nebraska. Increased lift from this shortwave has \nresulted in showers across mainly north central Kansas. These \nshowers are decreasing in coverage and are expected to continue to \ndecrease. Temperatures this afternoon across the area are expected \nto warm up into the mid 60s to lower 70s with southeast winds \ngusting up to around 20 to 25 MPH. Winds will weaken this evening \nand tonight with temperatures cooling into the 40s tonight into \nTuesday morning. \n\nAn upper level shortwave trough will pass over/near the area on \nTuesday. Rain chances (15% to around 25%) will increase Tuesday \nafternoon into Wednesday morning as a result of increased upper lift \nfrom the shortwave. Temperatures on Tuesday are expected to warm up \ninto the mid 60s to low 70s. Overnight low temperatures Tuesday \nnight will be in the 40s and 50s. The warm up continues into \nWednesday with high temperatures in the 70s. Low temperatures \nWednesday night/Thursday morning will be in the 50s areawide. \nTemperatures on Thursday will be similar or slightly warmer than \nthose on Wednesday as an upper ridge becomes more centered over the \narea and winds increase out of the south. Winds will be even \nstronger out of the south on Friday (gusts possibly reaching 35 to \n40 MPH in the afternoon) in advance of an approaching upper trough. \nHigh temperatures Friday are expected to be similar to those on \nThursday. Low temperatures Friday night will be in the 50s and 60s. \n\nThe upper trough will begin to pass over the region on Saturday with \na cold front moving into the area. This front will result in high \ntemperatures mainly in the 60s across the area on Sunday. This front \nwill also result in an increase in rain chances (up to 50% chance) \nbeginning on Saturday.",
